1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,459
Teacher midterm practice for C plus plus, question 16.

2
00:00:05,080 --> 00:00:10,234
Using an infinite loop, find the first positive integer k for which x to the k,

3
00:00:10,234 --> 00:00:15,506
over k factorial, is less than ten to the minus nine, with x equal to two.

4
00:00:15,506 --> 00:00:17,757
What k does this program print?

5
00:00:19,457 --> 00:00:25,029
Each pass, k goes up by one, and term is multiplied by x over k.

6
00:00:25,029 --> 00:00:28,415
That turns x to the k minus one over k minus one

7
00:00:28,415 --> 00:00:31,942
factorial into x to the k over k factorial.

8
00:00:32,562 --> 00:00:35,692
k equals one: two. k equals two: two.

9
00:00:35,692 --> 00:00:39,352
Then one point three three, then zero point six six seven.

10
00:00:39,352 --> 00:00:42,164
The terms grow at first, then shrink fast.

11
00:00:42,784 --> 00:00:45,715
At k equals sixteen, the term is three point one

12
00:00:45,715 --> 00:00:48,759
times ten to the minus nine: still too big.

13
00:00:49,379 --> 00:00:54,049
At k equals seventeen it is three point seven times ten to the minus ten.

14
00:00:54,049 --> 00:00:59,128
That is below the tolerance, so break, and the program prints seventeen.

15
00:01:00,728 --> 00:01:06,403
Two ideas. while of one with a break is the pattern for find the first k such that.

16
00:01:06,403 --> 00:01:10,323
And the update term times equals x over k builds each term from the

17
00:01:10,323 --> 00:01:14,653
last one, so the program never has to compute a huge factorial.

18
00:01:16,254 --> 00:01:18,724
So the program prints k equals seventeen.

19
00:01:19,544 --> 00:01:23,713
An off by one from incrementing k after the test instead of before it.

20
00:01:24,433 --> 00:01:28,738
Exam tip: for find the first k questions, check the two candidates on

21
00:01:28,738 --> 00:01:33,379
either side of the boundary, like sixteen and seventeen, to be sure.
